**Goal.**  
For a function \(f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z\) prove  

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\bigr)
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\Bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\Bigr)\;\lor\;
\Bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\Bigr).
\]

The proof is divided into two directions.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  From the functional equation to the two explicit forms  

Assume  

\[
\tag{H}\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 1.1  Rewriting the equation

*Put \(a=0\) in (H):*  

\[
f(0)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(b)\bigr)\qquad\forall b. \tag{1}
\]

Thus for every integer \(x\),

\[
f\bigl(f(x)\bigr)=f(0)+2f(x). \tag{2}
\]

*Put \(b=0\) in (H) and use (2):*  

\[
f(2a)+2f(0)=f\bigl(f(a)\bigr)=f(0)+2f(a),
\]
hence  

\[
\boxed{\,f(2a)=f(0)+2f(a)-2f(0)\,}\qquad\forall a. \tag{3}
\]

*Replace the right‑hand side of (H) by (2):*  

\[
f(2a)+2f(b)=f(0)+2f(a+b)\qquad\forall a,b. \tag{4}
\]

Now substitute (3) for \(f(2a)\) in (4) and cancel the common term \(f(0)\):

\[
\bigl(f(0)+2f(a)-2f(0)\bigr)+2f(b)=f(0)+2f(a+b)
\]
\[
\Longrightarrow\;2f(a)+2f(b)-2f(0)=2f(a+b).
\]

Dividing by \(2\) we obtain the **additive relation**

\[
\boxed{\,f(a+b)=f(a)+f(b)-f(0)\,}\qquad\forall a,b. \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 1.2  Passing to a genuine additive function  

Define  

\[
g(z):=f(z)-f(0)\qquad(z\in\mathbb Z).
\]

From (5) we have  

\[
g(a+b)=g(a)+g(b)\qquad\forall a,b, \tag{6}
\]

i.e. \(g\) is additive on \(\mathbb Z\).  
Moreover, (3) rewrites as  

\[
g(2a)=2g(a)\qquad\forall a. \tag{7}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 1.3  Determining the additive function on \(\mathbb Z\)

Let \(k:=g(1)\in\mathbb Z\).  
Using (6) we prove by induction on the natural number \(n\) that  

\[
g(n)=nk\qquad\forall n\ge 0.
\]

Indeed, the case \(n=0\) follows from \(g(0)=0\) (take \(a=b=0\) in (6)).  
Assume \(g(n)=nk\); then  

\[
g(n+1)=g(n)+g(1)=nk+k=(n+1)k.
\]

For a negative integer \(-n\) (\(n>0\)) we have  

\[
0=g(0)=g(n+(-n))=g(n)+g(-n)\;\Longrightarrow\;g(-n)=-g(n)=-nk,
\]

so the formula \(g(z)=kz\) holds for all \(z\in\mathbb Z\).

Consequently  

\[
\boxed{\,f(z)=kz+f(0)\,}\qquad\forall z\in\mathbb Z. \tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### 1.4  Determining the possible constants \(k\) and \(f(0)\)

Insert the linear expression (8) into the original equation (H).  
For arbitrary \(a,b\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{LHS}&=f(2a)+2f(b)\\
          &=\bigl(k(2a)+f(0)\bigr)+2\bigl(kb+f(0)\bigr)\\
          &=2k(a+b)+3f(0).
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{RHS}&=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\\
          &=f\bigl(k(a+b)+f(0)\bigr)\\
          &=k\bigl(k(a+b)+f(0)\bigr)+f(0)\\
          &=k^{2}(a+b)+k f(0)+f(0).
\end{aligned}
\]

Equality of the two sides for **all** integers \(a,b\) forces equality of the
coefficients of \((a+b)\) and of the constant term:

\[
\begin{cases}
2k = k^{2},\\[2mm]
3f(0) = k f(0)+f(0).
\end{cases}
\]

The first equation gives \(k(k-2)=0\); hence \(k=0\) or \(k=2\).

*If \(k=0\):* the second equation becomes \(3f(0)=f(0)\), i.e. \(2f(0)=0\),
so \(f(0)=0\).  From (8) we obtain \(f(z)=0\) for every \(z\).

*If \(k=2\):* the second equation reads \(3f(0)=3f(0)\), which holds for any
integer \(f(0)\).  Writing \(c:=f(0)\), (8) becomes  

\[
f(z)=2z+c\qquad\forall z.
\]

Thus from (H) we have proved  

\[
\forall z,\;f(z)=0\;\;\text{or}\;\;\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  From the explicit forms to the functional equation  

*Zero function.*  If \(f(z)=0\) for all \(z\), then both sides of (H) are
identically \(0\); the equation holds.

*Linear function.*  Suppose there exists \(c\in\mathbb Z\) such that
\(f(z)=2z+c\) for all \(z\).  Compute

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(2a)+2f(b) &= \bigl(2\cdot 2a + c\bigr)+2\bigl(2b + c\bigr)
            = 4a + c + 4b + 2c
            = 2\bigl(2(a+b)+c\bigr)+c,\\
f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr) &= f\bigl(2(a+b)+c\bigr)
                    = 2\bigl(2(a+b)+c\bigr)+c .
\end{aligned}
\]

Both expressions are equal, so (H) is satisfied.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Conclusion  

We have shown

* the functional equation implies that \(f\) is either the zero function or
  a function of the form \(z\mapsto 2z+c\);
* each of those two families indeed satisfies the functional equation.

Hence  

\[
\bigl(\forall a\,b,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f(f(a+b))\bigr)
\iff
\bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\bigr)\;\lor\;
\bigl(\exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\bigr).
\]

∎

</informal_proof>
