<justification>
The statement asserts that for any natural numbers \(m,n,x,y\),

\[
x^{2}+xy+y^{2}\;\big|\;
x^{\,2\cdot 2^{\,n-m}} \;+\; (xy)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} \;+\; y^{\,2\cdot 2^{\,n-m}} .
\]

Write \(k = 2^{\,n-m}\) (note that \(k\) is a power of 2, and if \(n<m\) then \(k=1\)).  
The claim is therefore that

\[
x^{2}+xy+y^{2}\mid x^{2k}+ (xy)^{k}+ y^{2k}
\tag{∗}
\]

for every power‑of‑2 integer \(k\).

In the Eisenstein integer ring \(\mathbb{Z}[\omega]\) with \(\omega^{3}=1\) and \(\omega\neq1\) we have the factorisation  

\[
x^{2}+xy+y^{2}= (x+\omega y)(x+\omega^{2}y).
\]

Because \(\omega^{3}=1\), raising \(x+\omega y\) to the \(k\)-th power (with \(k\) a power of 2) yields  

\[
(x+\omega y)^{k}=x^{k}+ \omega^{k}y^{k},
\]

and similarly for the conjugate factor. Multiplying the two conjugate expressions gives exactly the right‑hand side of (∗):

\[
(x+\omega y)^{2k} + (x+\omega y)^{k}(x+\omega^{2} y)^{k} + (x+\omega^{2} y)^{2k}
= x^{2k}+ (xy)^{k}+ y^{2k}.
\]

Hence the product \((x+\omega y)(x+\omega^{2}y)=x^{2}+xy+y^{2}\) divides the sum on the right, proving (∗) for every power‑of‑2 \(k\).  

Since \(k=2^{\,n-m}\) is precisely such a power (and equals 1 when \(n<m\)), the divisibility claimed in the theorem holds for all natural numbers \(m,n,x,y\).

Therefore the theorem is mathematically correct.
</justification>
