6 ^{2^{n}} &= \bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}},\\
9 ^{2^{n}} &= \bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Insert these equalities into `hfxn` :

\[
\begin{aligned}
f (2^{n})
&= 4 ^{2^{n}} + 6 ^{2^{n}} + 9 ^{2^{n}} \\
&= \bigl(4 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   + \bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   + \bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} .\tag{1}
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Factor a single copy of each base out of the three terms.

For any natural numbers `a` and `t` with `t > 0` we have  

\[
a^{t}=a\cdot a^{t-1}.
\]

Apply this to the three bases `4 ^{2^{m}}`, `6 ^{2^{m}}` and `9 ^{2^{m}}`
with the exponent  

\[
t:=2^{\,n-m} \;>\;0 .
\]

Thus

\[
\begin{aligned}
\bigl(4 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   &= 4 ^{2^{m}} \cdot \bigl(4 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1},\\
\bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   &= 6 ^{2^{m}} \cdot \bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1},\\
\bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   &= 9 ^{2^{m}} \cdot \bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}. \tag{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Introduce the three “quotients”.

Define

\[
\begin{aligned}
A &:= \bigl(4 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1},\\
B &:= \bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1},\\
C &:= \bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Each of `A`, `B`, `C` is a natural number because it is a power of a natural
number.

With the definitions above, the three equalities in (2) become

\[
\begin{aligned}
\bigl(4 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} &= 4 ^{2^{m}} \cdot A,\\
\bigl(6 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} &= 6 ^{2^{m}} \cdot B,\\
\bigl(9 ^{2^{m}}\bigr)^{\,2^{\,n-m}} &= 9 ^{2^{m}} \cdot C .\tag{3}
\end{aligned}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Substitute (3) into (1).

Using (3) we rewrite (1) as

\[
\begin{aligned}
