The hypotheses give  

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(2^m) &= a+b+c,\qquad 
a:=4^{2^m},\; b:=6^{2^m},\; c:=9^{2^m},\\[2mm]
f(2^n) &= a\cdot a^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+b\cdot b^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+c\cdot c^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Since \(a\cdot a^{k-1}=a^{k}\) (and similarly for \(b,c\)), the second hypothesis simplifies to  

\[
f(2^n)=a^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+b^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+c^{\,2^{\,n-m}}.
\]

The claimed conclusion is  

\[
f(2^n)= (a+b+c)\bigl(a^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+b^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+c^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}\bigr).
\]

Expanding the right‑hand side yields  

\[
a^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+b^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+c^{\,2^{\,n-m}}
   +\bigl(a b^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+a c^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+b a^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}+ \dots\bigr),
\]

which contains many cross terms (e.g. \(a b^{\,2^{\,n-m}-1}\)) that are **not** present in the expression obtained from the hypotheses. Because \(a,b,c>0\), these cross terms are non‑zero, so the two sides are generally different. Hence the equality asserted in the theorem does not follow from the given premises and is false in general.

A correct statement would be  

\[
f(2^n)=a^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+b^{\,2^{\,n-m}}+c^{\,2^{\,n-m}},
\]

or, written with the original notation,

\[
f(2^n)=
(4^{2^m})^{2^{\,n-m}}+(6^{2^m})^{2^{\,n-m}}+(9^{2^m})^{2^{\,n-m}}.
\]

With this modification the conclusion follows directly from the hypothesis `hrewrite`.
</justification>
